course: dictionary
lesson: 1803
title: Clotting: when something thickens
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Clotting is a word with two meanings, and the words around it tell you which one. When blood thickens into a lump at a cut so the bleeding stops, that is clotting. «Komodo dragons have venom glands in their jaws; the venom stops blood clotting and weakens prey.» Here the venom stops the blood from thickening, so the blood keeps flowing.
explain: Clotting can also mean any liquid, like cream or milk, going thick and lumpy. Look for clues: is the thing that clots a cut, blood, or a pot of cream? Ask yourself: what word would fit here? Then put your meaning into the sentence and check that it still makes sense.
explain: Clotting is also a verb: clot, clots, clotted, clotting. A cut can be clotting, and cream can have clotted. The same clue hunt works every time.
fact: Clotting means blood thickens into a lump so that bleeding stops.
fact: Tiny parts of blood called platelets help clotting, and clotting forms a scab over a cut.
fact: A «blood clot» inside a vein can be dangerous, so doctors watch for it.
fact: «Clotted cream», from Devon and Cornwall, is thick cream made by gentle heating.
fact: Clotting can also describe any liquid, like cream or milk, becoming thick and lumpy.
exercise: «Komodo dragons have venom glands in their jaws; the venom stops blood clotting and weakens prey.» What does the venom stop the blood doing? | Flowing into the jaws | *Thickening into a lump | Turning into cream | Going cold
exercise: A cut on Sam's knee is clotting. What is happening? | The cut is growing bigger | Sam is lying down | The knee is turning blue | *Blood is thickening to stop the bleeding
exercise: Mum says, «The cream has clotted in the pot.» What does clotted mean here? | Fizzy and cold | *Thick and lumpy | Sweet and sticky | Empty and clean
test: Which part of blood helps clotting and forms a scab over a cut? | *Platelets | Glands | Bones | Veins
test: A «blood clot» inside a vein can be dangerous. What do doctors do about it? | Ask for a holiday | Ignore it completely | Give the patient sweets | *Watch for it
exam: «Clotted cream» comes from which places? | London and Paris | Yorkshire and Kent | *Devon and Cornwall | Scotland and Wales
